%description:

Tcp.syncookiesAlways:on -- the cookie quantizes the MSS of the peer down to the table of Linux.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "a SYN cookie on every passive open: the cookie quantizes the MSS of the
peer down to the IPv4 msstab {536, 1300, 1440, 1460} of net/ipv4/syncookies.c".

The classes: off (false, the default), on (true). The code: with a cookie, the MSS of the peer
goes down to the IPv4 table {536, 1300, 1440, 1460} of Linux
(TcpConnection::processSegmentInListen()). This test separates "on" from "off".

Host under test: host2, the server, an echo server. Peer: host1 announces the MSS 1000 and
sends 3000 bytes. Observation: level 1, the first data segment of host2 carries 524 bytes:
the MSS 536 of the table less the 12 bytes of the timestamps.

%# parameter: Tcp.syncookiesAlways
%# class: on
%# value: true
%# key: *.host2.tcp.syncookiesAlways
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpSyncookiesAlwaysOn")
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
                  .first()
                  .assertPacket("pk.dataLength == tcp.headerLength + 524B")
                  .describe("the first data segment of host2, with 524 bytes")
                  .within(1.0));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpSyncookiesAlwaysOn compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host2.tcp.syncookiesAlways = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.mss = 1000
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 3000B
*.host2.app[0].echoFactor = 1
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpSyncookiesAlwaysOn: PASS
